# Destructuring a value that turns out to be an err.
#
# `halves 0` answers an err, and `pair a b = p` takes it apart. kanso check
# accepts the program (it reads calls, and `p` is a name). At run time:
#
#   interpreter:  error[runtime]: cannot destructure err "nothing to halve"
#                 as `pair`; bindings are irrefutable, so handle other types
#                 by dispatch first
#   native (kanso play, build, build --release):
#                 error[runtime]: the program ran out of stack: recursion
#                 went deeper than the stack holds
#
# Expected: the same diagnostic on every engine, and the interpreter's is the
# right one; there is no recursion here at all. (Better still, the err could
# ride the railway past the binding the way it rides past a call, or the
# checker could say that a binding pattern cannot take an err.) The decoder
# hit this on every corrupt input before its pattern reads moved into
# function heads.
#
# Run: kanso play destructured_err_overflows_stack.kso [--interp]
type pair
  left
  right

fn halves 0
  err "nothing to halve"

fn halves n
  pair (n / 2) (n - n / 2)

fn total n
  p = halves n
  pair a b = p
  a + b

print "{total 7}" .> (_ -> print "{total 0}")
